<!--
    IoT Sensor Telemetry Protocol
    Copyright(C) 2026 Matthew Gream (https://libiotdata.org)

    iotdata-fields-image.xml - the image field

    THIS IS THE SOURCE. iotdata_fields.h and iotdata_fields.c are generated from
    it by generators/iotdata-generator-fields.js through the templates in
    generators/templates, and editing them directly loses the edit at the next
    `make generate`.

    WHAT IS HERE AND WHAT IS IN THE TEMPLATE. This document carries what is true
    of a field in any language: its identifier, its quantities, their ranges and
    wire widths, the errors it can raise, its documentation -- and, for now, the
    bodies of its operations as literal C. The template carries what is true of
    the LANGUAGE: every brace, guard, typedef and macro form. So a toolchain that
    needs the C shaped differently is a template edit, not a generator change.

    <impl> IS LANGUAGE-SPECIFIC AND DELIBERATELY VERBATIM. Lifting the existing
    bodies in unchanged is the whole approach: nothing is re-derived, so nothing
    silently changes behaviour. A later <abstract> element may describe a
    quantisation rule declaratively, and the generator will use it only where no
    <impl> for that language and hook exists -- so a field migrates when someone
    chooses to migrate it, and until then the C that has been in service stays
    the definition.

    WHEN AN <impl> HAS A 'when' ATTRIBUTE it is selected by build configuration:
    'floating' and 'integer' are the two forms of iotdata_float_t. Several
    quantities need genuinely different arithmetic in the two, not the same
    expression at two widths, which is why they are separate bodies rather than
    one body and a cast.

    IDENTIFIERS are grouped in blocks of 16 per family, x0..x7 atomic and
    x8..xF composite. A convention only: identifiers are compared, never
    calculated with. 0x0000 is unassigned, 0x8000 and above belong to the
    implementer, 0xFFFF means none. TLV has no identifier.

    A small monochrome or greyscale picture, and the one field whose CODE is
    not generated: see iotdata_fields_image.h for why. This document carries
    everything about IMAGE that is not code -- its identifier, the quantities
    it puts on the wire, its errors and its documentation -- so that it appears
    in the registry and in the identifier-collision check alongside every other
    field, and so that there is one place to read what the field IS.
-->
<fields>

    <!-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
        IMAGE - a small picture, 1 to 4 bits per pixel, up to 254 bytes.

        THE FIRST VARIABLE-LENGTH FIELD in the protocol: an 8-bit length
        prefix, then a control byte packing format, size tier, compression and
        flags into two bits each, then the pixel data. Its wire width is
        therefore 0 in the registry, like the presence-masked arrays -- what it
        costs depends on what it carries.

        ITS CODE IS HAND-WRITTEN, in iotdata_fields_image.h, and that file
        explains the five structural reasons at length. The short version is
        that an encoder holds a const pointer to pixels it does not own while a
        decoder holds the 254 bytes itself, so the two contexts need different
        storage -- and a field whose shape depends on the direction it travels
        is not the shape the generator describes.

        NOTHING HERE IS CHECKED BY A COMPILER, which is the price of code="no":
        the five errors below were first written from memory and all five were
        wrong, and the registry printed the fiction without complaint. What
        keeps this document honest is tests/test_fields.c, whose IMAGE test
        names every error and every constant -- a wrong name there does not
        compile.

        WHAT STAYS HERE is everything that is not code. The identifier, so the
        collision check sees it. The quantities, so the registry lists what the
        field puts on the wire. The errors, so they are written down once. And
        this documentation, so a reader who wants to know what IMAGE is does not
        have to read its implementation to find out.
    -->    <field name="image" id="0x0090u" bits="0" code="no" mem="split">
        <documentation>
            <note for="field"><![CDATA[Field IMAGE]]></note>
        </documentation>
        <constants>
            <constant name="DATA_MAX" value="254 /* max pixel data after control byte */"/>
            <constant name="FMT_BILEVEL" value="0 /* 1 bpp */"/>
            <constant name="FMT_GREY4" value="1 /* 2 bpp */"/>
            <constant name="FMT_GREY16" value="2 /* 4 bpp */"/>
            <constant name="SIZE_24x18" value="0"/>
            <constant name="SIZE_32x24" value="1"/>
            <constant name="SIZE_48x36" value="2"/>
            <constant name="SIZE_64x48" value="3"/>
            <constant name="COMP_RAW" value="0"/>
            <constant name="COMP_RLE" value="1"/>
            <constant name="COMP_HEATSHRINK" value="2"/>
            <constant name="FLAG_FRAGMENT" value="(1U &lt;&lt; 1)"/>
            <constant name="FLAG_INVERT" value="(1U &lt;&lt; 0)"/>
            <constant name="HS_WINDOW_SZ2" value="8 /* 256-byte window */"/>
            <constant name="HS_LOOKAHEAD_SZ2" value="4 /* 16-byte lookahead */"/>
        </constants>
        <!-- bilevel, 4-level grey or 16-level grey -->        <quantity name="image_pixel_format" type="uint8_t" param="pixel_format">            <check error="FORMAT_HIGH" text="Image pixel format above 2"/>            <check error="SIZE_HIGH" text="Image size tier above 3"/>            <check error="COMPRESSION_HIGH" text="Image compression above 2"/>            <check error="DATA_NULL" text="Image data pointer is NULL"/>            <check error="DATA_HIGH" text="Image data exceeds 254 bytes"/>        </quantity>
        <!-- one of four fixed pixel geometries -->        <quantity name="image_size_tier" type="uint8_t" param="size_tier">        </quantity>
        <!-- raw, run-length or heatshrink LZSS -->        <quantity name="image_compression" type="uint8_t" param="compression">        </quantity>
        <!-- fragment and invert -->        <quantity name="image_flags" type="uint8_t" param="flags">        </quantity>
        <!-- the pixels; a const pointer when encoding, a 254-byte buffer when decoding -->        <quantity name="image_data" type="uint8_t" param="data">        </quantity>
        <!-- how many of those bytes are real -->        <quantity name="image_data_len" type="uint8_t" param="data_len">        </quantity>
    </field>

</fields>
